@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Cost
+
+A cost proof states an estimate priced by the unit table and is red when a measured size costs more than the estimate allows, by tolerances derived from the table's own measured spread. A proof is an ordinary deftest calling ```seon.cost/proof```, so it sits in a task's proofs beside the behaviour proofs; the estimate's shape is the `:seon.cost/estimate` schema, and what each contracted function cost comes from `seon.profile`.
+
+## The unit table
+
+```seon.cost/units``` holds what one unit operation costs on this runtime: ns and bytes per unit, and `:range`, the least and largest ratio to that price of every measurement taken of it. Each row's bench is in the test, ```seon.cost-test/unit-benches```, measured through ```seon.cost-test/measure-unit```; ```seon.cost-test/table``` measures every row k times at 1k, 10k and 100k units and answers the table's literal (each row priced at its median 10k measurement, ranged by ```seon.cost-test/unit-range```). The table holds the release binary's 10k median, ranged over both the release binary and source mode, since proofs run in both. Bytes are ranged only for rows that allocate.
+
+A unit stated as a difference (`:minus`, e.g. ``:db/pull-attr``, a two-attribute pull minus a one-attribute pull) measures both terms on one input, the second right after the first, so only the difference in work remains. Measured on two different dbs, that row was bimodal: its one entity sat in a ~519-datom index, at the edge of a 512-datom EAVT leaf, and read 8.8 KB in one index level or 11.6 KB in two, on one binary (lane UNITFLAKE, 2026-10-21; task `seon.cost/a-unit-measures-the-work-not-the-cache`). Both pull rows now take one setup, ```seon.cost-test/notes-db```: a branch of the test store with n notes. An extra attribute measures ~10 KB, ten times its estimate; that is Datahike's pull, filed as `datahike.pull-api/a-pull-allocates-what-it-reads`.
+
+`seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` re-measures every row at 10k: bytes inside the row's own range, wall time inside the table's pooled range. `seon.cost-test/a-unit-measures-the-same-across-runs` holds the pull rows to 2% across fresh branches, order and size.
+
+## The judge's tolerances
+
+No tolerance is a guessed constant: ```seon.cost/tolerances``` derives them per estimate from the table's ranges, and every verdict carries them as `:tolerances`.
+
+- **Bytes** are counted. The slack is the largest ratio measured among the rows the estimate's `:ops` price, or the whole table's when it prices none or states bytes in `:per-n`.
+- **Wall time** pools every row's range, since three runs of one row do not show the machine's spread (3.15x when derived).
+- **Growth**: a dimension is STEEPER past `:growth` plus the steepest `log(hi/lo)/log(100)` of the same rows, which is how fast a unit's own cost moved over the table's 100x span (```seon.cost/table-span```).
+- **Calls** are exact: 1.0x, and no exponent tolerance.
+- **An accepted overrun** (`:overrun`, naming its open task) holds while the largest size is within the dimension's slack of what was measured when its task was filed.
+
+```seon.cost/judge``` applies them, `seon.cost/explain` and the verdict's render (`seon.render.ui/verdict-lines`, the page's box and the model's cost report alike) print them, and the agent's prompt (`seon.context/system-prompt`) renders the widest through ```seon.cost/tolerance-text``` and ```seon.cost/widest-tolerances```, so no copy of a number goes stale when the table is re-measured. Proofs: `seon.cost-test/the-exponent-tolerance-follows-slack-and-span`, `seon.cost-test/slack-is-the-units-measured-spread`, `seon.context-test/the-prompt-states-the-judges-own-tolerances`.
